stdout:
{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A **diffraction grating** uses many closely spaced slits to spread light waves and let them combine.\nDiffraction is the spreading of a wave beyond its straight-ahead path after an opening or an edge."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "A transmission grating has many narrow, parallel slits separated by opaque strips, as in Figure 206.\nThe slits transmit light, while the opaque strips block it."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Light from all the slits combines to determine the pattern of brightness.\nThe magnifying glass makes the closely spaced parallel strips easier to see;"
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "it does not add another set of slits.\nThe source describes a special ruling machine making parallel lines on a glass plate, with several thousand lines in every 1 millimeter."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "The total can exceed 100000 lines, giving the grating many repeating parts.\nThe source also describes a white-gelatin grating impression held between two glass plates."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "A grating can use reflected light instead of transmitting light through glass.\nThe source calls **reflection gratings** the highest-quality type and describes alternating reflecting and scattering strips."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 6,
      "text": "The scattering strips are cut into a polished metal plate with a cutting tool.\nTransmission and reflection are different arrangements, but repeated strips allow waves from different positions to combine."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Grating spacing and wavelength.** Let $a$ be the width of a transparent slit and $b$ the width of the opaque interval beside it.\nFor a reflection grating, these are the widths of the reflecting and scattering strips."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Their sum, $d=a+b$, is the **grating spacing**, also called the grating constant.\nIt is the distance from a position in one slit to the corresponding position in the next slit."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "It is not just the width of the opaque interval.\nNow let parallel, monochromatic light of wavelength $\\lambda$ fall on the grating in Figure 207."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "Wavelength is the distance over which the wave repeats along its direction of travel.\nThink of the distance between neighboring positions at the same stage of the wave."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "Monochromatic means a single wavelength in this model, and parallel means the incoming directions are the same.\nThe diagram shows light arriving perpendicular to the grating."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "The following derivation uses this normal incidence and uniformly spaced slits.\nGrating spacing belongs to the apparatus, wavelength belongs to the light, and the distance to the screen is a different distance."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Path difference.** Treat positions across the slits as sources of secondary waves spreading in different directions.\nThese secondary waves describe how the incoming light propagates;"
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "the slits are not new lamps.\nConsider a direction at angle $\\varphi$ to straight ahead, the direction perpendicular to the grating."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The diagram shows parallel rays from corresponding edges of neighboring slits.\nThey travel different distances before reaching the same comparison wavefront."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The extra distance is the length of segment $AC$, called the path difference.\nIf $AC$ is an integer number of wavelengths long, one wave travels an extra whole number of cycles."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "The waves still arrive at the same stage of oscillation, or the same phase, so they reinforce one another.\nThis uses the common incoming beam and regular spacing, not unrelated light sources."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "A and B mark corresponding edges of adjacent slits.\nThe helper line from B to C is perpendicular to the outgoing rays, and C lies on the ray from A.\nThis makes the right triangle $ABC$, from which we can find the length of the leg $AC$ representing the path difference."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The path difference is the projection of the spacing between adjacent slits onto the outgoing direction.\nIn a right triangle, sine is the ratio of the opposite leg to the hypotenuse, so:\n$|AC|=|AB|\\sin\\varphi=d\\sin\\varphi$."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Here the grating spacing is the hypotenuse length, and the path difference is the leg opposite the viewing angle.\nLooking straight ahead gives no extra path length between the two rays."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Looking to the side produces a path difference.\nAn angle is not a distance;\nsine lets us use the angle to find a length ratio."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Principal maxima and orders.** In certain directions the waves reinforce one another, making prominent bright bands, or intensity maxima.\nWrite the angle of such a direction as $\\varphi$."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Requiring the path difference to be a whole number of wavelengths determines angle $\\varphi$:\n$d\\sin\\varphi=k\\lambda$ (7.9)."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Here $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$, and these maxima are called **principal maxima**.\nThe order counts the whole wavelengths in the path difference, so it is an integer rather than any continuously variable number."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The zero order points straight ahead and has no path difference.\nThe first order has an extra wavelength, and the second order has two."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "The source lists nonnegative orders for the center and one side.\nUnder the same incidence, matching directions occur on the other side."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "An order is geometrically allowed only when the relation gives a real propagation direction, since the magnitude of sine cannot exceed one.\nOrders therefore cannot increase without limit;\nthe available orders depend on both grating spacing and wavelength."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Condition (7.9) applies beyond the lower slit edges drawn in the diagram.\nPoints at matching positions within neighboring slits are separated by $d$."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Their secondary waves also have path difference $k\\lambda$, so they reinforce one another.\nEach position across one slit has a corresponding position across the next slit, and light from the whole slit contributes."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "This is why the edge triangle represents the reinforcement condition between different slits.\nWaves from different positions within a single slit must also combine, however, and this affects the strength of each order."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "Some geometrically allowed orders may be weak or even canceled by single-slit diffraction.\nThe principal-maximum condition does not guarantee equally bright orders."
    },
    {
      "index": 32,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Bright bands on the screen.** Place a converging lens behind the grating and put the screen in its focal plane.\nThe focal plane contains the points where the lens brings parallel rays from each direction together."
    },
    {
      "index": 33,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "It is a specific plane, not an arbitrary screen position.\nThe lens brings rays traveling in the same outgoing direction to the same point on the screen, where their waves combine."
    },
    {
      "index": 34,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The angles $\\varphi$ satisfying condition (7.9) determine the positions of the corresponding bright bands.\nThe lens changes the later paths of the rays;"
    },
    {
      "index": 35,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "it does not turn slit spacing into the spacing between bands on the screen.\nA direction farther from straight ahead reaches a position farther from the center in the focal plane.\nFinding an actual distance on that screen also requires the lens focal length, not just the grating spacing."
    },
    {
      "index": 36,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**White light and spectra.** White light contains light of different wavelengths.\nExcept for the central maximum corresponding to $k=0$, maximum positions depend on wavelength, so a grating separates white light into a spectrum (color plate IV,1)."
    },
    {
      "index": 37,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The path difference is zero straight ahead, allowing different wavelengths to reinforce in the central direction.\nThe zero order therefore does not separate colors as the side orders do."
    },
    {
      "index": 38,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "For the same grating and the same order, a larger $\\lambda$ puts the maximum farther from the central maximum (color plate IV2,3).\nKeep the order fixed when comparing wavelengths;"
    },
    {
      "index": 39,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "bands of different orders are not the same comparison.\nEach allowed nonzero value of $k$ has its corresponding spectrum, while the central zero order combines wavelengths in one bright band."
    },
    {
      "index": 40,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "Spectra of different orders can overlap in an observation.\nThat does not mean the grating changes one wavelength into another.\nThe color-plate citations refer to the original book, not to a newly supplied modern plate."
    },
    {
      "index": 41,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Measuring wavelength.** A diffraction grating can measure wavelength precisely.\nWith the grating spacing known, measure the angle $\\varphi$ of a principal maximum and identify its order."
    },
    {
      "index": 42,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The earlier relation then determines the wavelength.\nThe angle gives the propagation direction, while the identified order tells how many wavelengths fit the path difference."
    },
    {
      "index": 43,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The central band cannot distinguish wavelengths this way, because every wavelength points straight ahead in the zero order.\nUse an identifiable nonzero order and the incidence conditions of the derivation."
    },
    {
      "index": 44,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Grating effects in everyday objects.** The source uses the gaps between eyelashes as an example of a rough grating.\nIt describes rainbow-like colors seen while squinting at a bright source, caused by diffraction and separation of white light around the eyelashes."
    },
    {
      "index": 45,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "This describes an observation, not a reason to stare at an intense light.\nThe source also compares the closely spaced grooves of a well-used record to a reflection diffraction grating."
    },
    {
      "index": 46,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Reflected lamp light can show a spectrum, and sometimes several spectra corresponding to different values of $k$.\nThe source says the pattern is especially clear when the lamp light strikes the record at a relatively large incidence angle."
    },
    {
      "index": 47,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "That is an oblique-incidence observation, so the earlier normal-incidence relation cannot be applied to it without accounting for the changed incidence.\nThe grooves distribute reflected light among directions;\nthe colored light is not newly created by the record."
    }
  ],
